      "A website is not a brochure you print once. It is software, running on servers, talking to other software, with a security certificate that expires, dependencies that age, and a contact form that can break silently on a Friday night. The question is never whether a website needs maintenance — it is whether you pay a little for it every month, or a lot for it all at once when something breaks. This guide sets out what website maintenance really costs in 2026, what a care plan should actually cover, and how much maintenance your site needs.",
      "## The short answer",
      "Published maintenance pricing in 2026 clusters roughly here: Freelancer care for a WordPress site: $50–$200 a month — Updates, backups, and a check that the site is still up; Agency care for a custom or Next.js site: $499–$1,500 a month — Monitoring, security updates, backups, fixes, and someone accountable when it breaks; Full care with feature time: $1,500–$3,999 a month — Everything above, plus ongoing improvements, priority response, and AI agent monitoring for larger systems.",
      "Our own Care retainers are published at $499 a month for Basic, $1,499 for Standard, and $3,999 for Premium — the same ladder for websites and for the custom systems we build. The rest of this guide explains what sits behind those numbers, and what to demand from any care plan at any price.",
      "## What breaks when nobody is watching",
      "Certificates expire. An SSL certificate lasts 90 days to a year depending on how it is issued, and when it lapses your site shows a security warning to every visitor. Most modern hosting renews certificates automatically — until the renewal fails silently, which is exactly how it usually fails.",
      "Dependencies age. A modern website is built on dozens of libraries, and each one gets security fixes over time. A site that is never updated accumulates known vulnerabilities, and the fix is never cheaper than the update would have been. This is the maintenance item most often skipped, and the one that produces the most expensive emergencies.",
      "Forms stop working. A contact form depends on an email service, a spam filter, and a destination inbox. Any of the three can change without telling you, and the result is the same: enquiries vanish. A business that discovers its form has been broken for a month has lost a month of leads, and no amount of apologies recovers them.",
      "Content goes stale. Prices change, team members leave, services are added, and the site keeps saying the old thing. Stale content does not break the site — it quietly breaks trust, and it is the maintenance task only the owner can do, because only the owner knows what changed.",
      "Backups are the test. A backup that has never been restored is a hope, not a backup. Care means backups that are tested, stored separately from the site, and recent enough that losing a week of content is the worst case, not the expected one.",
      "## What a care plan should actually cover",
      "Before comparing prices, check that each plan answers the same questions. Uptime monitoring: is someone watching whether the site is up, around the clock, and will they tell you before your customers do? Backups: how often, stored where, and tested by actually restoring them? Security updates: are dependencies and the platform updated on a schedule, not just when something breaks?",
      "Bug fixes: when something breaks, who fixes it, how fast, and is the fix included or billed separately? A plan that charges extra for every fix is a retainer in name only. Content updates: can you send a price change or a new team photo and have it live without a new invoice? Performance: is someone watching load times, and will they act when the site slows down?",
      "And reporting: a monthly report that says what was done, what was fixed, what was updated, and what needs your decision. A care plan with no reporting is a direct debit with good intentions.",
      "## How much maintenance does your site need?",
      "A simple brochure site with a contact form needs the least: monitoring, backups, certificate checks, and dependency updates — the Basic end of the ladder. A site with a blog, a booking flow, or a store needs more: the moving parts multiply, and so do the ways they can break. A site with customer accounts, payments, or integrations needs the most: logins, data, and other people's systems all need watching, and the cost of a failure is measured in customers, not embarrassment.",
      "The planning rule for custom systems is to budget 15–25% of the build cost per year for maintenance. A $30,000 system therefore costs roughly $4,500–$7,500 a year to run properly, before hosting. A brochure website is cheaper than that rule suggests; a platform is more expensive. Wherever your site sits, a plan that costs nothing is a plan that does not exist — the maintenance is happening or it is not, and the bill arrives either way.",
