      "A platform store — Shopify, WooCommerce, or similar — is fast and cheap to start because the hard parts already exist: the checkout, the payment handling, the admin panel, the security. You are paying for setup and design, not for reinventing a cart. It is the right buy for a new store testing demand, for a catalogue under a few hundred products, and for any business that wants to be selling next month rather than next quarter.",
      "A custom store is the right buy when the platform starts fighting you: a catalogue that needs its own structure, a checkout flow that is part of the product, pricing or fulfilment rules no plugin handles cleanly, or integrations with your stock, accounting, or supplier systems. The build costs more because you are paying for software, not setup — but the monthly platform bill drops, and the store does exactly what your business does, instead of approximately what it does.",
      "The mistake is choosing by build price alone. A platform store that costs $3,000 to build and $300 a month in platform and app fees costs $6,600 in year one and $3,600 every year after. A custom store that costs $12,000 to build and $200 a month to host and maintain costs $14,400 in year one and $2,400 a year after. By year three, the custom store is cheaper — and the platform store has paid its app subscriptions every month whether it sold anything or not.",
      "## The costs nobody puts in the quote",
      "Payment fees. Every order pays the payment gateway — typically around 2–3% plus a fixed fee per transaction, depending on the provider and the country. On $10,000 of monthly sales, that is $200–$300 a month, every month, before you have paid for anything else. It is not a hidden fee, but it is the largest ongoing cost of most small stores, and it belongs in the business plan, not in a footnote.",
      "Apps and plugins. The platform store that looks complete on day one usually needs paid apps for the second month: reviews, search, discounts, email, shipping rules, each $5–$50 a month. App creep is the quiet budget killer of platform stores — audit the subscriptions every quarter, and cancel anything the store has outgrown.",
      "Product content. Photography, descriptions, and variants are the real work of an online store, and they are rarely in the build quote. A hundred products photographed and described properly is a project in itself, and a store with poor product content will not sell no matter how good the design is.",
      "Fulfilment and returns. Packing, shipping, and handling returns cost money and time on every order. A store that sells well and fulfils badly loses customers faster than a store that sells slowly — and the fulfilment workflow deserves the same attention as the checkout.",
      "## Year one vs year three",
      "Year one is the expensive year for a custom store and the cheap year for a platform store. Year three reverses it. The question to ask is not which store is cheaper, but which store matches how long you expect to sell online: if this is a test, buy the platform store and prove the demand. If this is the business, buy the store that fits the business, and budget for maintenance from day one — 15–25% of the build cost per year is the planning rule for systems, and a store with payments and customer data is a system.",
      "Whichever route you choose, the build is only part of the cost. Budget for the first year honestly: build, platform, payment fees at your expected volume, apps, product content, and maintenance. A quote that only shows the build price is a quote that has not finished.",
      "## Frequently asked questions",
      "How much does an ecommerce website cost in 2026?",
      "A DIY platform store costs roughly $350–$1,000 a year plus payment fees. An agency-built platform store costs $3,000–$12,000 to build plus $30–$300 a month in platform and app fees. A custom ecommerce build costs $8,000–$30,000 or more to build, with lower ongoing platform costs.",
      "Is Shopify or a custom store better for a small shop?",
      "Shopify is better for testing demand and for catalogues under a few hundred products — it is faster and cheaper to start. A custom store is better when the catalogue, checkout, or integrations need something the platform cannot do cleanly. Most small shops should start on a platform and move to custom when the platform starts costing more than the build would have.",
      "What are the ongoing monthly costs of an online store?",
      "The platform subscription, paid apps, payment gateway fees of around 2–3% per order, and maintenance. A small store typically pays $30–$300 a month in platform and app fees, plus payment fees that scale with sales, plus maintenance from $499 a month on a care plan.",
      "How long does it take to build an online store?",
      "A platform store typically takes 3–6 weeks from content to launch; a custom store takes 8–16 weeks. The timeline stretches when product content — photography, descriptions, variants — arrives late, which is the most common delay in ecommerce builds.",
      "Do I need a custom store if I only sell a few products?",
      "Almost certainly not. A handful of products, a simple checkout, and standard shipping are exactly what platforms are built for. A custom store earns its price when the catalogue, the checkout flow, or the integrations are unusual — not when the product count is small.",
      "If you are weighing a store, send us the catalogue — or just tell us what you sell, how many products, and how orders work today. We will reply within one business day with an honest view of whether a platform store or a custom build fits, what each would cost over three years, and what we would leave out of version one. You can also write to us directly at hello@cinem.tech, or explore our website development service first.",
